  { "ref": "Ex 7.1 IV Q1", "verdict": "NO-KEY", "book_key": "(none printed)", "our_answer": "\\delta = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}", "derivation": "The printed EXERCISE 7.1 key carries blocks I, II and III only; part IV (epsilon-delta) has no entry, as expected for a construct-a-delta question. Our answer is self-verifying: |(2x+3)-7| = 2|x-2| < \\varepsilon exactly when |x-2| < \\varepsilon/2." },
  { "ref": "Ex 7.1 IV Q2", "verdict": "NO-KEY", "book_key": "(none printed)", "our_answer": "\\delta = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{3}", "derivation": "No printed key for Ex 7.1 IV. Self-verifying: |(3x+2)-(-7)| = 3|x+3| < \\varepsilon exactly when |x+3| < \\varepsilon/3." },
  { "ref": "Ex 7.1 IV Q3", "verdict": "NO-KEY", "book_key": "(none printed)", "our_answer": "\\delta = \\min\\{1, \\varepsilon/5\\}", "derivation": "No printed key for Ex 7.1 IV. Self-verifying: with |x-2|<1 we get 1<x<3 so |x+2|<5, hence |x^2-4| = |x-2||x+2| < 5|x-2| < \\varepsilon when |x-2| < \\varepsilon/5. The min with 1 is required to license the |x+2|<5 bound." },
  { "ref": "Ex 7.1 IV Q4", "verdict": "NO-KEY", "book_key": "(none printed)", "our_answer": "\\delta = \\min\\{1, \\varepsilon/4\\}", "derivation": "No printed key for Ex 7.1 IV. Self-verifying: with |x-1|<1 we get 0<x<2 so |x+2|<4, hence |x^2+x-2| = |x-1||x+2| < 4|x-1| < \\varepsilon when |x-1| < \\varepsilon/4." },

